# Contact read of a CB/Visa card through its Payment System Environment.
#
# THIS CARD DOES NOT FOLLOW THE SPECIFICATION, and the capture is kept exactly
# as it was recorded rather than adjusted to keep a test passing.
#
# It served the directory at P1='00' and answered '6985' at P1='01':
#  - ISO/IEC 7816-4:2005 section 7.3.2 : "Each record number or identifier is a
#    number from one to 254, encoded by a value of P1 from '01' to 'FE'. Zero
#    (encoded '00') is reserved for special purposes." P1='00' is therefore not
#    a record number at all, it designates the current record, and selecting by
#    short EF identifier has just reset the record pointer.
#  - EMV 4.3 and 4.4 Book 1 section 12.3.2 step 2 : the terminal "reads all the
#    records in the Payment System Directory beginning with record number 1 and
#    continuing with successive records until the card returns SW1 SW2 =
#    '6A83'". Answering '6985' for record 1 is neither the record nor the end
#    of the directory.
#  - EMV 4.3 Book 1 section 12.3.2 step 1 gives the terminal its conduct: on
#    any other status word it clears the candidate list and restarts with the
#    List of AIDs method of section 12.3.3.
#
# The session below was recorded by an earlier, 0 based reading. A conforming
# terminal asks record 1 first, receives the '6985' and never reaches the
# directory entry, so this trace can no longer drive a full read. It is used to
# assert exactly that give up, see EmvParserTest#testPSE.
#
